.Dd October 15, 2026
.Dt FIRETOWER 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm firetower
.Nd show which processes use the CPU, and who started them
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl hV
.Op Fl n Ar rows
.Op Fl s Ar seconds
.Op Ar rows
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
samples the system for two seconds (see
.Fl s )
and prints one screen about where the CPU time went.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h , Fl Fl help
Print a usage summary.
.It Fl n Ar rows
List this many of the busiest processes.
The default is 12.
A bare
.Ar rows
operand does the same.
.It Fl s Ar seconds
Sample over this many whole seconds.
The default is 2.
A longer window smooths out bursts.
.It Fl V , Fl Fl version
Print the version.
.El
.Ss The header
The first line gives the load averages over one, five and fifteen minutes,
and the one-minute load divided by the number of cores.
Above 1.0, more work is waiting than the cores can run.
.Pp
Next come the share of CPU time spent in programs
.Pq user ,
in the kernel on their behalf
.Pq sys ,
and idle; then memory: the total, what the kernel has wired, what it holds
compressed, and how much it counts as free; then, on a laptop, the battery
or AC state, the charge and the time left.
Below 120 columns these are printed on separate lines.
.Ss Notes
Lines starting with
.Ql ->
say what the numbers add up to:
.Bl -bullet
.It
saturated: less than 10% idle;
.It
sys above user, and at least 25%: the kernel is doing most of the work;
.It
memory pressure: less than 20% free by the kernel's own measure;
.It
thermal: the CPU speed is limited;
.It
a process using at least 25% CPU has run for over an hour under a Claude
Code session that is now idle, so it may have been left behind.
.El
.Ss The table
Each busy process gets a row.
.Sy %CPU
is over the sampling window; 100% is one full core.
It is red from 100% and yellow from 50%.
.Sy AGE
is how long the process has run.
The last three columns give context:
.Bl -tag -width "LAUNCHED BY"
.It Sy WHERE
The working directory, usually the repository or worktree.
.It Sy WHAT
What it runs: a JVM main class or jar, a script's file name, a
subcommand.
For a Claude Code session, its state and title.
.It Sy LAUNCHED BY
The parent, then the nearest coding agent above it, with that session's
state and title, and its directory when that differs.
.El
.Pp
A coding agent is a registered Claude Code session or a process named aider,
amp, claude, codex, cursor-agent, gemini, goose or opencode.
Only Claude Code sessions show a state and title.
.Pp
Below 120 columns the context moves to an indented line under each row.
.Ss Totals
.Sy by program
adds up the CPU of every process with the same name and lists the eight
largest, so many small processes are not missed.
.Sy agents
does the same for coding agents, when they did not make that list.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
.It Ev CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
Where Claude Code keeps its state.
The default is
.Pa ~/.claude .
.It Ev COLUMNS
The terminal width, overriding what the terminal reports.
.It Ev NO_COLOR
When set to a non-empty value, output is not colored.
Output is never colored when it is not a terminal.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.claude/sessions/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa .json
Claude Code's registry of live sessions, read for a session's state and
name.
.It Pa ~/.claude/projects/*/ Ns Ar session Ns Pa .jsonl
Session transcripts.
Only title records in the last megabyte are read.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Invalid options exit with status 2.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Take a look:
.Dl $ firetower
.Pp
Show only the five busiest processes:
.Dl $ firetower 5
.Pp
List more of them over a smoother ten-second sample:
.Dl $ firetower -n 25 -s 10
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fs_usage 1 ,
.Xr pmset 1 ,
.Xr powermetrics 1 ,
.Xr ps 1 ,
.Xr top 1 ,
.Xr lsof 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Oskar Bo\(:ethius Lissheim
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
runs on macOS only.
.Pp
Without root, the working directory of another user's process is not shown.
.Pp
Claude Code's session files are internal and undocumented.
If their format changes, sessions are shown by name and process ID alone.
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Command lines often carry tokens and passwords, and a process can rewrite
its own.
.Nm
reads them only to find a hint and never prints one whole.
Program names come from the kernel, which a process cannot rename; a name
the kernel cuts at 16 characters is completed from the path of the
executable the kernel ran.
.Pp
A hint is a dotted name (a script, class or module) or a short lowercase
subcommand with at most two digits, such as
.Ql test
or
.Ql dev:ram .
It is never taken from inline code such as
.Ql bash -c
or
.Ql node -e .
A secret passed as the first argument and shaped like a subcommand, such as
.Ql hunter2 ,
still passes, since nothing tells the two apart.
.Pp
Session titles are printed as Claude Code wrote them.
.Nm
makes no network connections and writes no files.
